--   (A.58), OA p.35 — the long-run average cost of the barrier policy is $\gamma$ (corrected: barrier policy almost surely)
-- statement:
--   Let $(f,B,\gamma)$ solve (13) and let $(W,I,E,a,S)$ be the barrier policy, driven by a Brownian motion $X$ with drift $0$ and variance $\Sigma$. Then
--   $$\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac1t\,\mathbf E\Big[\sum_ib_i\int_0^t(a_i(s)-S_i)^+\,ds+c_k\mu_kE(t)\Big]=\gamma.$$
--   This is the upper half of the optimality proof.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: barrier policy: C1–C3 for every path of X + θe (Definition 6, Lemma 4) → C1–C3 for almost every ω; (10b)–(10f) for every ω; reason: printed Lemma 4 is false: n=2, μ=(1,1), λ=(1,2), b=(1,3/2), c=(3/5,3/5), S=0, any h>0, θ≤0, Σ>0, so that bjμj/λj=3/4>3/5=ckμk. For (f,B,γ) of Lemma 2 Part ii, f′ rises strictly from 0 to 3/5 on [0,B], so f′(w₀)=1/2 at one w₀∈(0,B), and β(w)=w on [0,w₀), β(w)=2w on [w₀,B]. For x(t)=w₀/2+(3/2)w₀t no triple satisfies C1–C3 with b=B: w climbs to w₀, then the slope is at most −w₀/2 at or above w₀ and at least w₀/2 below it, so w can neither leave w₀ nor stay there
--   - reading: q* is "an optimal solution to (14)" (Definition 2), κ(w) chosen so that β is right continuous (OA p.26) → q* fixed: the fill of OA A.2 for w ≤ Σᵢ Sᵢ/μᵢ, otherwise κ with largest λ, then least index; reason: Definition 2 leaves ties open; this is the choice the paper names on OA pp.2 and 26
--   - reading: (10f): a(·,W(·)) is 𝔽-adapted → progressive measurability, and integrable paths (aᵢ − Sᵢ)⁺ on every [0,t]; reason: the paper integrates these paths in (10a)–(10b) and applies Tonelli in (A.47), which needs both
--   - encoding: a(t,W(t,ω),ω) → one process a t ω; reason: the allocation is used only along the path
--   - encoding: real-valued expectations in (10a) → ℝ≥0∞ with lower integrals; reason: the integrands are nonnegative and an infinite expectation stays infinite
--   - generalization: Σ := Σᵢ αᵢλᵢ(1+σᵢ²)/μᵢ² (p.15) → Σ > 0 a free parameter; reason: every statement depends on Σ only through Σ > 0; the source value is one such Σ
--   - encoding: SKU set {1,…,I}, indices j, k → Fin n with n ≥ 1; bjμj/λj and ckμk as minima; reason: only the values of the minima enter the statements
--   - encoding: f ∈ C₂ with domain ℝ₊ → f : ℝ → ℝ, C² on ℝ, with (13) imposed for w ≥ 0; reason: a C² function on [0,∞) extends to ℝ, so no solution is lost
--   - generalization: 𝔽 generated by (Xᵢ) → any filtration for which X is a Brownian motion; reason: the natural filtration is one such filtration
--   - encoding: C1's integral ∫₀ᵗ (drift)(w(y)) dy and C3: ∫₀^∞ w dℓ = ∫₀^∞ (b − w) du = 0 → the drift path y ↦ β(w(y)) interval-integrable on every [0,t]; dℓ, du as Stieltjes measures of ℓ, u extended by 0 to t < 0, giving no mass to {w > 0} resp. {w < b} on [0,∞); reason: β∘w is bounded and measurable for càdlàg w with values in [0,b], so integrability excludes no triple; since w ∈ [0,b] the two vanishing integrals say exactly that these sets are null
